WHY AGPL AND NOT MIT

This library exists so that a platform can collect a payment for a worker and
have it land in the worker's own bank account, without the platform ever
holding the money. MIT would let anyone take that code, close it, and sell it
back to the same workers' employers with no obligation to anyone.

AGPL-3.0 is OSI-approved and genuinely open source: you may read, run, modify
and redistribute this software. What it adds is section 13 — if you run a
MODIFIED version of this software as a network service, you must offer your
users the complete corresponding source of your version, under this same
licence.

Someone may fork this and compete; they may not fork it, close it, and compete.

